# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-exe-suffix-callers-not-swept — the producer gained .exe, its callers did not
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: alfe-sbcl-windows-build-missing-exe-suffix
#+BRANCH: disable-arm32-release

* Symptom

Both Windows CAD benchmarks failed on the =release-1.9.0= tag pipeline
(jobs 16026668742 and 16026668743):

#+begin_example
/bin/sh: .../autolisp-front-end/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl:
cannot execute binary file: Exec format error
make: *** [Makefile:207: run-bricscad-windows] Error 126
#+end_example

Not a CAD problem, not a benchmark problem: the file that name refers to
does not exist under that name any more.

* Cause — a fix whose consumers were never swept

=alfe-sbcl-windows-build-missing-exe-suffix= fixed the PRODUCER:
=generate.lisp= now asks =save-lisp-and-die= for the =.exe= suffix
explicitly on Windows, instead of trusting the implementation to add it.
Correct, and it is why =build:alfe:windows= works.

But renaming what a build produces breaks every caller that spells the
old name, and nobody went looking for those. =autolisp-benchmark/Makefile=
spelled the extension-less name:

: ALFE_SBCL := $(ALFE_ROOT)/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl

On POSIX that is the file. On Windows it is nothing — and an
extension-less name is not executable there at all, which is what
=Exec format error= is saying.

This is the shape of defect that only appears on the one platform nobody
runs locally, and it appeared on a RELEASE tag rather than on a merge
request, because the Windows benchmarks are the last jobs to run.

* Fixed here

=autolisp-benchmark/Makefile= gains the same =EXE= mechanism
=autolisp-front-end/Makefile= already carries — copied rather than
reinvented, so the two agree:

: HOST_UNAME ?= $(shell uname -s)
: EXE := $(if $(filter MINGW% MSYS% CYGWIN%,$(HOST_UNAME)),.exe,)
: ALFE_SBCL := $(ALFE_ROOT)/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-sbcl$(EXE)
: ALFE_CCL  := $(ALFE_ROOT)/tools/alfe/bin/alfe-ccl$(EXE)

=HOST_UNAME= is a =?== on purpose: it is what lets the Windows branch be
checked from a POSIX host, which is the only way to check it without the
machine. Verified both ways:

: make -C autolisp-benchmark -n run-bricscad-windows HOST_UNAME=MINGW64_NT-10.0
:   -> alfe-sbcl.exe
: make -C autolisp-benchmark -n run-bricscad-macos
:   -> alfe-sbcl

* Not verified

That the benchmarks then SUCCEED on Windows. This corrects the name they
invoke; whether =benchmark:autocad:windows= has a further problem is the
open half of =benchmark-autocad-windows-hangs-and-blocks-release= (it has
never succeeded, and option 4 there is still open).
=benchmark:bricscad:windows= used to finish in 1 m 37 s, so that one is
expected to come back.

* The general point, worth keeping

A build that changes the NAME of what it produces has to be followed by a
sweep of who says that name. Two callers were already correct because
they were written after the rename — =scripts/run-pathname-probe.ps1= and
=scripts/run-path-sysvars-probe.ps1= both copy to =.exe= when the file
lacks it — which is precisely why the survivor was easy to miss: the
places a person looked at recently were fine, and the one nobody had
touched in months was not.

*The sweep found a SECOND caller, and my first reading of it was wrong.*
I wrote that =.gitlab/native.yml= passes =ALFE_BIN= extension-less in two
places but that both hand it to =run-*.ps1= scripts which do the =.exe=
dance themselves. Only one does. =harvest:sysvars:bricscad:windows=
passes it straight to =make save-sysvars ALFE="$env:ALFE_BIN"=, so
nothing downstream repairs the name, and it would fail exactly like the
benchmarks. Fixed here too: that job now names =alfe-sbcl.exe=. The
macOS twin keeps the extension-less name, which is right there.

Swept after that correction: no other Makefile or script spells
=bin/alfe-sbcl= without either =$(EXE)=, an explicit =.exe=, or a
copy-to-=.exe= step. =scripts/run-pathname-probe.ps1= and
=scripts/run-path-sysvars-probe.ps1= are the two that do the dance.

That near-miss is the argument for writing this ticket rather than just
fixing the Makefile: the first caller was found by a failing job, the
second only by looking, and a claim that the sweep was complete is worth
nothing until the sweep has actually been run.

* Acceptance criteria

- =benchmark:bricscad:windows= runs the benchmark instead of dying on
  =Exec format error= (it is the one with a known-good baseline).
- No change to POSIX behaviour, which the =make -n= check above asserts.

* Verification (2026-09-13) — fixes present, Windows benchmark still UNCONFIRMED

Both consumer fixes are confirmed present on master (=e719c575=):
- =autolisp-benchmark/Makefile= carries the =HOST_UNAME=/=EXE= mechanism
  (lines 67-70: =ALFE_SBCL := …/alfe-sbcl$(EXE)=), identical to
  =autolisp-front-end/Makefile=, so the benchmark spells =alfe-sbcl.exe= on
  Windows and =alfe-sbcl= on POSIX.
- =harvest:sysvars:bricscad:windows= names =alfe-sbcl.exe= (ALFE_BIN,
  .gitlab/native.yml:716); the POSIX =make -n= check in this issue still
  asserts no POSIX regression.

Not confirmed, so this stays open: the acceptance criterion is that
=benchmark:bricscad:windows= actually RUNS instead of dying on
=Exec format error=, and that job has not run since the fix —
=benchmark:bricscad:windows= is =manual= (runner offline) on the release
tags checked (=release-1.9.3=), so the name fix has not been exercised
end-to-end on the Windows/BricsCAD runner. With the Windows runner now
available (pjb): trigger =benchmark:bricscad:windows= (known-good ~1 m 37 s
baseline) on a master or tag pipeline to confirm and close. Not done here
because it consumes the exclusive CAD Windows runner (~30 min, a laptop
sometimes on battery per TRIAGE) — pjb's call, not a background action.
(The =benchmark:autocad:windows= half remains separately blocked by
=benchmark-autocad-windows-hangs-and-blocks-release=.)
